To transform Tokens Studio design tokens into CSS while resolving aliases, you must use the @tokens-studio/sd-transforms integration package with Style Dictionary [1][2][3]. 

First, install the package and register the transforms in your configuration script [4]. This enables Style Dictionary to parse Tokens Studio JSON, which uses different syntax (`$value`, `$type`) and reference patterns (`{path.to.token}`) than the default [4].

// Import and register transforms
import StyleDictionary from 'style-dictionary';
import { registerTransforms } from '@tokens-studio/sd-transforms';

registerTransforms(StyleDictionary);

const sd = new StyleDictionary({
 source: ['tokens//*.json'],
 platforms: {
 css: {
 transformGroup: 'tokens-studio',
 buildPath: 'build/css/',
 files: [{ destination: 'variables.css', format: 'css/variables' }]
 }
 }
});

await sd.buildAllPlatforms;

When generating CSS, if you want your output to retain references as CSS variables (e.g., `--color-text: var(--color-blue)` instead of the resolved hex value), set the `outputReferences` option to `true` in your platform configuration [5][6]. Note that using `outputReferences` with transitive transforms like `ts/resolveMath` can cause resolution conflicts, as Style Dictionary may struggle to replace reference placeholders if the token values have been transformed [7][6]. If you encounter this, you may need to apply a custom `calc()` transform to wrap math operations [7][6].

