In Tailwind CSS v4, you manage design tokens using the @theme directive [1][2]. To implement layered theming and dark mode, use a two-stage approach: define your raw semantic values in:root and theme-specific selectors (like.dark), then map them to utility classes via the @theme directive [3].

Avoid using @theme inline for themeable colors, as it bakes values into utilities at build time, preventing runtime switching [3]. Instead, use the standard @theme block to create semantic references [3]:

@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark,.dark *));:root {
 --color-background: #ffffff;
}.dark {
 --color-background: #000000;
}

@theme {
 --color-background: var(--color-background);
}

This setup allows you to use utility classes like bg-background that update automatically when the dark class is toggled [4][3]. Organize your tokens by role (e.g., surface, foreground) rather than specific values to ensure your system remains resilient to rebrands or new themes [5][6][2]. Place these theme overrides within an @layer theme block to ensure proper CSS specificity [7][8].

